-----------
LISP Manual
-----------
Everything is an expression. Expression is either an atom or a list. An atom is a sequence of symbols.
A list is a sequence of expressions separated by a space and wrapped in parentheses. The first element
of a list (a. k. a. head) is an operator, and the remaining ones (a. k. a. tail) are the arguments. 

Here are all the predefined primitive operators:

(1) (quote x) returns x "as is". 'x is a syntactic sugar for quoting x.
(2) (atom x) returns t if x is an atom. Otherwise returns () (an empty list). Atoms t and () are
used to represent true and false values respectively.
(3) (eq x y) returns t if x and y are the same atom, and () otherwise.
(4) (car x) expects x to be a list and returns its head.
(5) (cdr x) expects x to be a list and returns its tail (also a list).
(6) (cons x y) expects y to be a list and returns a list where value of x is head and value of y is
tail.
(7) (cond (p1 e1) ... (pn en)) evaluates pk until it returns t at some k. Then ek is returned as a value
of an expression.
(8) (lambda (p1 .. pn) e) returns an operator that takes n parameters, substitutes them into expression e
and evaluates the whole thing.
(9) (label f (lambda (p1 ... pn) e)) is same as (8) but now the operator gets a name f. It makes it
possible to have operator used inside itself, thus giving a recursion.

Not enough? This whole thing is Turing complete, so you have theoretically got everything you may need.
Happy LISP hacking!
